WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:09.233
Welcome back. In this video, we learn how to divide one polynomial by another, using a method you already know from primary school: long division.

2
00:00:09.633 --> 00:00:19.633
We cover two lessons. First, the division itself. Then, how the quotient and the remainder fit together in one simple equation, which even lets us work backwards.

3
00:00:20.833 --> 00:00:30.700
Imagine you run a food delivery app. As it becomes popular, the number of daily orders grows faster and faster. A curve like that is described by a polynomial with a high power.

4
00:00:31.100 --> 00:00:39.367
Meanwhile, you hire new riders at a steady pace. The same number of extra riders every week. That is a straight line, a linear polynomial.

5
00:00:39.767 --> 00:00:48.900
So how many orders must each rider cover? To find out, we divide the total orders by the number of riders. One polynomial, divided by another.

6
00:00:49.300 --> 00:00:57.800
That is polynomial division. A tool for sharing a big, fast growing quantity among smaller units. And it works just like dividing whole numbers.

7
00:00:59.000 --> 00:01:07.733
Let's warm up with numbers. Seventy-nine divided by three. The divisor, three, sits outside, and the number being divided goes inside.

8
00:01:08.133 --> 00:01:20.300
Step one, divide. Three goes into seven, two times. Step two, multiply. Two times three is six. Step three, subtract. Seven minus six leaves one.

9
00:01:20.700 --> 00:01:32.467
Step four, bring down the next digit, the nine, to make nineteen. Then repeat. Three goes into nineteen, six times. Six times three is eighteen. Nineteen minus eighteen leaves one.

10
00:01:32.867 --> 00:01:40.633
We stop here, because what is left, one, is smaller than the divisor, three. So the quotient is twenty-six, and the remainder is one.

11
00:01:41.033 --> 00:01:47.933
Divide, multiply, subtract, bring down. Remember that rhythm, because polynomials follow exactly the same steps.

12
00:01:49.133 --> 00:01:56.667
Now the real thing. Divide two X cubed, minus five X squared, minus two, by two X squared, minus X, minus one.

13
00:01:57.067 --> 00:02:07.067
Write both in order, from the highest power down. Notice that the dividend has no X term. So we leave a gap there, to keep every power in its own column.

14
00:02:07.467 --> 00:02:15.733
Step one, divide. But only the leading terms. Two X cubed, divided by two X squared, is X. Write X on top.

15
00:02:16.133 --> 00:02:27.600
Step two, multiply. X times the whole divisor gives two X cubed, minus X squared, minus X. Write it underneath, each term in its matching column.

16
00:02:28.000 --> 00:02:41.367
Step three, subtract. The X cubed terms cancel, which was the whole point. Negative five X squared, minus negative X squared, is negative four X squared. And nothing, minus negative X, is positive X.

17
00:02:41.767 --> 00:02:50.033
Bring down the negative two, and go again. Negative four X squared, divided by two X squared, is negative two. Write it on top.

18
00:02:50.433 --> 00:02:56.600
Multiply. Negative two times the divisor is negative four X squared, plus two X, plus two.

19
00:02:57.000 --> 00:03:04.300
Subtract. The X squared terms cancel. X minus two X is negative X. And negative two, minus two, is negative four.

20
00:03:04.700 --> 00:03:14.300
Can we keep going? No. What is left has degree one, but the divisor has degree two. Once the degree of what is left is lower than the degree of the divisor, we stop.

21
00:03:14.700 --> 00:03:18.800
So the quotient is X minus two, and the remainder is negative X minus four.

22
00:03:20.000 --> 00:03:29.600
Sometimes a second letter shows up. Divide four X squared, minus nine A X, minus nine A squared, by X minus three A, treating both as polynomials in X.

23
00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:40.267
The trick is simple. X is our variable, and the letter A is treated just like a number. So nine A X counts as an X term, and nine A squared is the constant term.

24
00:03:40.667 --> 00:03:54.133
Divide the leading terms. Four X squared, divided by X, is four X. Multiply, to get four X squared, minus twelve A X. Subtract, and three A X is left. Then bring down the negative nine A squared.

25
00:03:54.533 --> 00:04:04.633
Again. Three A X, divided by X, is three A. Multiply, to get three A X, minus nine A squared. Subtract, and nothing is left at all.

26
00:04:05.033 --> 00:04:11.133
The quotient is four X plus three A, and the remainder is zero. A zero remainder means the division is exact.

27
00:04:12.333 --> 00:04:23.000
On to the second lesson. How do the four pieces of a division fit together? Go back to numbers. Seven divided by three gives a quotient of two, and a remainder of one.

28
00:04:23.400 --> 00:04:29.733
Which means seven equals three times two, plus one. Two full groups of three, and one left over.

29
00:04:30.133 --> 00:04:39.600
Polynomials obey exactly the same rule. If A divided by B gives a quotient Q and a remainder R, then A equals B times Q, plus R.

30
00:04:40.000 --> 00:04:47.200
There is one condition. The degree of the remainder must be lower than the degree of the divisor. Otherwise, the division is not finished.

31
00:04:47.600 --> 00:04:56.733
This equation is a perfect way to check your work. In our first example, multiply the divisor by the quotient, add the remainder, and the dividend comes back.

32
00:04:57.933 --> 00:05:12.267
The same equation lets us work backwards. A mystery polynomial A is divided by X squared, minus two X, minus one. The quotient is X plus three, and the remainder is negative two X plus five. What is A?

33
00:05:12.667 --> 00:05:16.133
Simply substitute into A equals B Q plus R.

34
00:05:16.533 --> 00:05:31.733
Now expand. X squared, times X plus three, gives X cubed plus three X squared. Negative two X gives negative two X squared, minus six X. Negative one gives negative X, minus three. Then the remainder comes along.

35
00:05:32.133 --> 00:05:45.100
Finally, collect like terms. The X squared terms make X squared. The X terms make negative nine X. And the numbers make two. So A is X cubed, plus X squared, minus nine X, plus two.

36
00:05:46.300 --> 00:05:58.867
Now the other way round. Four X cubed, plus eight X, minus nine, is divided by a mystery polynomial B. The quotient is two X minus three, and the remainder is X plus fifteen. Find B.

37
00:05:59.267 --> 00:06:02.800
Substitute again. This time, the unknown is the divisor.

38
00:06:03.200 --> 00:06:12.333
First, subtract the remainder from both sides. Eight X minus X is seven X. Negative nine minus fifteen is negative twenty-four.

39
00:06:12.733 --> 00:06:21.533
To get B alone, divide by two X minus three. That is a long division, and now you know how. Do not forget the gap for the missing X squared term.

40
00:06:21.933 --> 00:06:31.233
Out comes two X squared, plus three X, plus eight, with a remainder of zero. And here it has to be zero. If it is not, there is a mistake somewhere.

41
00:06:32.433 --> 00:06:45.400
Before we finish, four traps to avoid. Trap one. Sign errors when subtracting. Subtracting a negative term means adding. Negative five X squared, minus negative X squared, is negative four X squared, not negative six.

42
00:06:45.800 --> 00:06:54.933
Trap two. Multiplying by only the leading term of the divisor. The new quotient term must multiply the entire divisor, every single term.

43
00:06:55.333 --> 00:07:01.900
Trap three. Stopping too early. Keep going until the degree of the remainder is lower than the degree of the divisor.

44
00:07:02.300 --> 00:07:09.833
Trap four. Believing the remainder must always be zero. It can be any polynomial, as long as its degree is lower than the degree of the divisor.

45
00:07:11.033 --> 00:07:22.100
Let's recap. Polynomial long division repeats four steps. Divide the leading terms, multiply, subtract, and bring down. Leave gaps for missing powers, and treat other letters as constants.

46
00:07:22.500 --> 00:07:28.500
And every division satisfies A equals B Q plus R, where the remainder has a lower degree than the divisor.

47
00:07:28.900 --> 00:07:40.600
Now it is your turn. Pause the video, and divide two X cubed, minus thirteen X, plus one, by X squared, plus two X, minus three. Watch out for the missing term.

48
00:07:41.000 --> 00:07:47.967
Did you get a quotient of two X minus four, and a remainder of X minus eleven? Well done. See you in the next video.

